  "query_preview": "..\nEasterly low level flow off the Atlantic is resulting in\nconsiderable cloud cover, mist and fog along and east of the\nAppalachians this morning. This shallow layer of moisture is\nexpected to dissipate, leaving us with mostly sunny skies this\nafternoon under dry High Pressure.\n\nThe next several days will continue to remain rather quiet \nweather wise, with upper level ridging and surface high pressure\nremaining fixed over the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic states. A\nfront crossing the Great Lakes will fade and lose its identity\nas it passes over the northern Mid-Atlantic tonight. Its passage\nwill bring winds around to the west-northwest for Friday, but no\nsignificant airmass change. If anything the west wind will\nprovide a boost in temperature with downslope adiabatic warming\nhelping to push the temperatures well into the 70s Friday with\nurban areas east of the mountains reaching 80 deg F. \n\nFor Halloween, upper level clouds look to push into the region \nfrom the next system that looks to move across the continental\nU.S.(CONUS). This should bring temperatures back down to near \nnormal. Regardless, dry weather will continue through the end \nof the month leading to pleasant trick-or-treat weather for all\nthe ghosts and goblins. \n\nUncertainty continues to develop with forecast Sunday and Monday\nfor what had been looking like a potential rain maker. Latest \n00Z GFS and ECMWF have trended much drier with respect to the \ninitial southern stream wave early in the week and now focusing\non a mid or late week opportunity for wetting rainfall. \n\nWith the exception of some mid-upper level cloud cover, both \nthe GFS and ECMWF keep the region predominantly dry through next\nTuesday as high pressure to our north looks to maintain enough \ndry air across the region within the low levels to prevent \nprecipitation from falling. Any threat before then looks minimal\nand mainly confined to areas west of the Blue Ridge. \n\nBy the end of next week, there appears to be decent ensemble\nsupport in the GEFS and EPS ensembles for an amplified upper \nlevel trough digging south into the Gulf states before lifting \nto the northeast through the region by the Thursday/Friday \ntimeframe. Timing and specifics on this system are still in\nquestion; however, widespread rainfall may be on tap for parts\nof the region for the end of next week as this system evolves.",
